window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["24"]={"https://theconversation.com/a-new-democratic-congress-will-view-trumps-harsh-cuba-policy-skeptically-but-that-doesnt-mean-a-change-is-coming-soon-296017":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:30Z","sha":"81026daed5f52a8c2938f03ee8387396b69432c03bd76cfdbacc5becf329de97","read":"https://theconversation.com/a-new-democratic-congress-will-view-trumps-harsh-cuba-policy-skeptically-but-that-doesnt-mean-a-change-is-coming-soon-296017","bytes":195105,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"A new Democratic Congress will view Trump’s harsh Cuba policy skeptically – but that doesn’t mean a change is coming soon","by":[{"n":"Joseph J. Gonzalez","r":"Associate Professor of Global Studies, Appalachian State University"}],"pub":"2026-11-14T10:45:46Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["The so-called “ ",["blue tsunami","https://www.axios.com/2026/11/04/midterm-takeaways-blue-wave-trump-democrats"],"” that hit the United States in the midterm elections is causing ripples around the world."]},{"k":"p","s":["Having won a historic victory, congressional Democrats now appear poised to challenge the ",["Trump administration’s conduct of foreign affairs","https://www.reuters.com/world/us/five-ways-us-foreign-policy-may-change-with-democratic-congress-2026-11-04/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["Iran war","https://theconversation.com/with-control-of-congress-heres-how-democrats-could-stop-trumps-war-in-iran-295954"],", ",["support for Ukraine","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/8/democrats-congressional-win-could-strengthen-ukraines-hand-against-russia"]," and the ",["management of Venezuelan oil","https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/05/trump-election-energy-venezuela-critical-mineral-renewable-iran.html"]," certainly all appear to be on the agenda of some Democrats."]},{"k":"p","s":["The flipping of the House and Senate from Republican to Democratic control has also led to speculation that members of Congress may also wish to challenge ",["Trump’s Cuba policy","https://responsiblestatecraft.org/trump-marco-rubio-cuba-invasion/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["After all, the United States’ position on Cuba has swung in recent decades, depending on the balance of power in Washington. An ",["easing of tensions","https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/node/352651"]," and normalization of relations between the U.S. and Cuba took place under the presidency of Barack Obama, only ",["to be reversed","https://ballotpedia.org/Federal_policy_on_Cuba,_2017"]," following the election of Donald Trump in 2016. President Joe Biden ",["largely followed Trump’s lead","https://www.cfr.org/articles/uely-biden-trump-throughline-cuba"],", while ",["making more allowance","https://www.wola.org/analysis/biden-administration-takes-positive-steps-on-cuba/"]," for activities supporting the Cuban people."]},{"k":"p","s":["In his second term, however, Trump has pursued an even harder line than before. Since January 2026, the administration has instituted a devastating economic and fuel blockade, prohibiting Cubans from ",["importing oil","https://www.cfr.org/articles/trumps-maximum-pressure-campaign-on-cuba-explained"],". This pressure tactic is meant to get the Cuban government to ",["agree to increased economic privatization","https://www.cfr.org/articles/trumps-maximum-pressure-campaign-on-cuba-explained"]," and political ",["democratization","https://www.cfr.org/articles/trumps-maximum-pressure-campaign-on-cuba-explained"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["While the Cuban government has met ",["certain demands","https://bisi.org.uk/reports/cuba-seeks-to-deter-the-us-with-economic-reform-can-it-succeed"],", notably with respect to the economy, it has resolutely ",["refused to change","https://www.miamiherald.com/news/nation-world/world/americas/cuba/article315378763.html"]," either its personnel or its authoritarian character."]},{"k":"p","s":["The result of this blockade has been a stalemate and increased hardship for Cubans. As a ",["historian of U.S.-Cuban relations","https://interdisciplinary.appstate.edu/faculty/dr-joseph-gonzalez"],", I saw that hardship firsthand ",["during a visit to Havana in June 2026","https://theconversation.com/we-are-waiting-for-the-americans-to-save-us-in-crisis-cubans-have-given-up-on-reform-from-within-285324"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["While I understand the notion that emboldened Democrats may try to force a change in direction of the Trump administration’s foreign policy, including on Cuba, I doubt they will be able to do so. In fact, there are reasons to believe that the midterm results may actually reinforce Republicans’ hard-line approach to the Caribbean island."]},{"k":"h","s":["Strained life in Cuba"]},{"k":"p","s":["Since 1961, at the height of the Cold War, the U.S. government has enforced a ",["rigorous sanctions","https://nsarchive.gwu.edu/briefing-book/cuba/2022-02-02/cuba-embargoed-us-trade-sanctions-turn-sixty"]," regime against communist Cuba – the ",["longest-running","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_embargo_against_Cuba"]," embargo in modern history."]},{"k":"p","s":["In January 2026, the Trump administration made that embargo even more severe with the oil importation ban, part of a regional pressure campaign that also saw U.S. forces abduct and remove from office Venezuela’s President ",["Nicolás Maduro","https://www.state.gov/nicolas-maduro-moros"],", a longtime ally of the Cuban government."]},{"k":"p","s":["For Cubans, no oil means ",["no electricity","https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/jun/08/cuba-power-outages-electricity"],". Blackouts, ",["already a problem","https://theconversation.com/cubas-leaders-see-their-options-dim-amid-blackouts-and-a-shrinking-economy-266203"],", ",["have become longer","https://www.usnews.com/news/world/articles/2026-08-07/as-blackouts-stretch-for-days-life-in-cuba-revolves-around-small-bursts-of-electricity"]," and more frequent."]},{"k":"p","s":["Absent electricity, Cubans have no regular access to ",["running water","https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/jul/26/we-count-every-litre-how-cubas-energy-crisis-sparked-a-daily-struggle-for-water"],". Disease, from mosquitoes, other pests and unsafe water, has ",["increased dramatically","https://cu.usembassy.gov/health-alert-u-s-embassy-havana-cuba-september-4-2026/"],". Cuba’s once much-praised medical system often ",["cannot treat","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/longform/2026/7/30/to-play-god-cubas-healthcare-system-collapses-under-us-pressure"]," patients unless they bring their own drugs and supplies."]},{"k":"p","s":["Inflation ",["has soared","https://translatingcuba.com/cuba-is-4th-worst-in-the-world-in-inflation-surpassed-only-by-venezuela-north-korea-and-iran/"]," and tourists have ",["stopped coming","https://apnews.com/article/cuba-tourism-visitor-decrease-us-energy-embargo-515d283e68ce317e3de7c01daab70fd7"],", while ",["industrial","https://translatingcuba.com/eclac-forecasts-a-10-3-drop-in-the-cuban-economy-in-2026/"]," and ",["agricultural","https://ukragroconsult.com/en/news/crops-rot-in-the-fields-in-cuba-and-farmers-sell-land-due-to-fuel-shortages/"]," production has declined dramatically."]},{"k":"p","s":["Cubans hate the U.S. embargo for contributing to this misery. At the same time, many ",["also blame","https://theconversation.com/we-are-waiting-for-the-americans-to-save-us-in-crisis-cubans-have-given-up-on-reform-from-within-285324"]," their government. During my last visit, Cubans I spoke with told me that they believe their leaders are more concerned with protecting their power and privileges than with the welfare of the average Cuban."]},{"k":"p","s":["For many Cubans, this indifference explains why President Miguel Díaz-Canel and his allies have rejected the Trump administration’s central demand of surrendering their monopoly on power."]},{"k":"h","s":["Havana’s strategy"]},{"k":"p","s":["Trump’s demands, blockade and the threat of military force have led to some concessions by Cuba’s leaders."]},{"k":"p","s":["During the past year, Cuba’s government has released some ",["political prisoners","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cwy3r3w4zl8o"]," and ",["permitted greater","https://www.hospitalityinvestor.com/investment/cuba-tries-save-its-tourism-industry-permitting-100-cent-foreign-ownership"]," foreign investment, most notably from ",["Cuban Americans","https://www.nbcnews.com/world/cuba/cuba-allow-nationals-living-abroad-invest-businesses-island-economy-rcna263637"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The government in Havana has also ",["expanded the range","https://horizontecubano.law.columbia.edu/news/tremors-cubas-private-sector-and-new-business-architecture"]," of legally permitted private enterprise in Cuba."]},{"k":"p","s":["But when it comes to the question of power, Díaz-Canel and his allies remain implacable. They know that relinquishing power will cut off the supply of profits from ",["state-owned businesses","https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/what-is-gaesa-which-has-taken-center-stage-us-cuba-tensions-2026-05-21/"]," that flows their way and maintains their ",["opulent lifestyles","https://www.orlandosentinel.com/2026/04/23/the-colonel-with-a-rolex-and-a-yacht-raul-castros-grandson-holds-key-to-cuba-deal/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["They also argue that Trump has no ",["right to determine","https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/cubas-president-says-island-does-not-wish-for-u-s-aggression-but-ready-to-fight-if-necessary"]," the rulers of a sovereign nation."]},{"k":"h","s":["Rubio’s ideological priority"]},{"k":"p","s":["For the moment, it appears that Díaz-Canel hopes international and domestic politics ",["will compel","https://saisreview.sais.jhu.edu/strategic-procrastination-how-the-cuban-regime-uses-calculated-delay-to-ensure-its-survival/"]," Trump to turn his attention from Cuba."]},{"k":"p","s":["No doubt, the “blue wave” of the U.S. midterms has encouraged that belief. If so, I believe the hopes of the Cuban government are probably misplaced."]},{"k":"p","s":["This is in large part because U.S. Secretary of State Marco Rubio, a ",["Cuban American","https://www.state.gov/biographies/marco-rubio/"]," who formerly represented Florida in the U.S. Senate, ",["remains determined","https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/oct/23/marco-rubio-cuba-trump"]," to topple the Cuban government."]},{"k":"p","s":["Rubio has long been a foreign policy ",["hawk","https://www.cnas.org/publications/commentary/marco-rubio-is-rebooting-the-neocons-for-the-maga-era"]," and fervent ",["proponent","https://www.npr.org/2026/05/22/nx-s1-5830999/rubio-diplomacy-cuba-trump-military-action"]," of regime change in Cuba. As secretary of state, he has also been a key figure in the Trump administration’s push to expand ",["American power","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/1/28/rubio-testifies-before-us-senate-on-abduction-of-venezuelas-maduro"]," across Latin America and support ",["right-wing","https://www.npr.org/2026/09/11/nx-s1-5964731/rubio-visits-new-right-wing-governments-in-latin-america"]," movements and governments."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is also no secret that Rubio ",["wishes to succeed","https://www.cnn.com/interactive/politics/2028-presidential-candidates-vis/marco-rubio/"]," Trump as president. That has not changed since the midterm elections."]},{"k":"p","s":["In order to become the Republican nominee for president, Rubio will ",["most likely need to win","https://floridapolitics.com/archives/832278-ron-desantis-catches-up-with-marco-rubio-in-2028-gop-prediction-markets/"]," Florida’s primary. And if he is to win the White House, Rubio ",["will surely need","https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/florida-stayed-redbut-changed-the-math-for-2028-d1898e05"]," Florida’s electoral votes, especially in light of the results of last week’s election in Texas, which saw a U.S. Senate seat and the governorship turn Democrat."]},{"k":"p","s":["Rubio’s path to the presidency will be easier if he continues to enjoy the support of South Florida’s Cuban American community, which favors regime change by an ",["overwhelming margin","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ckrerylrlg9vo"]," and represent a powerful voting bloc in Florida."]},{"k":"p","s":["In short, I believe ambition and ideology will provide Rubio with the impetus to refuse all compromise with what remains of Cuba’s communist government."]},{"k":"p","s":["Interestingly, some Republicans seem uneasy with ",["aspects","https://www.csmonitor.com/World/Americas/2026/0921/cuba-trump-midterms-mineral-deposits"]," of America’s Cuba policy, particularly the emphasis on investment opportunities as opposed to democratic change."]},{"k":"p","s":["Nonetheless, Trump has reasons to push harder on Cuba. The midterm results show that his domestic agenda – and the war in Iran – are vote losers. He may see toppling the Cuban government as a guaranteed “win” for him and his administration, both in terms of legacy and in the eyes of voters."]},{"k":"h","s":["Congressional opposition?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Of course, Democrats in the new Congress ",["could deny the administration funds","https://www.commondreams.org/news/groups-urge-cuba-war-powers-resolution"]," to maintain the oil blockade or, in an extreme case, invade Cuba."]},{"k":"p","s":["Democrats could also ",["exercise Congress’ powers","https://www.news10.com/news/politics/ap-analysts-weigh-how-democrats-midterm-wins-could-shift-trumps-latin-america-policy/"]," of investigation and oversight."]},{"k":"p","s":["Though these measures enjoy ",["some support","https://www.bellyofthebeastcuba.com/could-a-democratic-congress-stop-trumps-blockade-on-cuba"]," among Democrats, it is far from certain that House or Senate leaders would support them."]},{"k":"p","s":["After all, the Democratic Congress has ",["other priorities","https://abcnews.com/Politics/democrats-house-heres-plan-investigate-legislative-agenda/story?id=137832144"]," that appear of greater concern to the American people, such as the Iran war, not to mention domestic policy issues, such as healthcare and an “ ",["affordability agenda","https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/04/congressional-democrats-affordability-oversight.html"],".”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Moreover, Democratic leaders may regard efforts to end the oil embargo ",["as politically risky","https://responsiblestatecraft.org/trump-marco-rubio-cuba-invasion/"],": Such efforts could alienate voters in Florida and divide the Democratic caucus, some of whose members favor the embargo."]},{"k":"p","s":["Outside of Florida, Americans ",["generally disapprove","https://yougov.com/en-us/articles/54332-more-americans-disapprove-than-approve-of-the-us-blocking-oil-shipments-to-cuba-and-the-us-embargo"]," of U.S. policy toward Cuba. But they are unlikely to demand that their representatives force the administration to change course."]},{"k":"p","s":["With the pressure on Democrats over Cuba relatively low, and their priorities elsewhere, the Trump administration will likely face little opposition to maintaining the embargo until it can say that it has achieved its political and strategic objectives."]},{"k":"p","s":["Meanwhile, members of Cuba’s governing elite, without appealing alternatives, will likely look to cling to power as long as they can."]},{"k":"p","s":["And the Cuban people will continue to pay the price."]}],"chars":7725},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/libya":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:03Z","sha":"5af58f90bba7072b25c05c50eb4975c2bbedca07d9f1409c113cc054ccb396e9","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/libya","bytes":57985,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Libya travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-03T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-07-21T10:00:09+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel insurance could be invalidated if you travel against advice from the Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Areas where FCDO advises against travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to Libya except for the cities of:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Tripoli"]},{"k":"li","s":["Benghazi, Al-Bayda and Derna, and the surrounding area known as Jebel Akhdar, either side of the coastal and desert roads between and including the towns of Al-Maqrun and Al-Tamimi"]},{"k":"li","s":["Misrata"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the cities of:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Tripoli"]},{"k":"li","s":["Benghazi, Al-Bayda and Derna, and the surrounding area knows as Jebel Akhdar, either side of the coastal and desert roads between and including the towns of Al-Maqrun and Al-Tamimi"]},{"k":"li","s":["Misrata"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["why the FCDO advises against travel","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/libya/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["UK government support"]},{"k":"p","s":["UK government support is limited in Libya. If you are in Libya and need urgent help from the UK government (for example, if you’ve been attacked, arrested or someone has died) ",["contact the British Embassy in Tunis, Tunisia","https://www.gov.uk/world/organisations/british-embassy-tunis"]," who provide assistance for British nationals in Libya. In-person support is not available in Libya. If you are a British national already in, or planning to travel to Tripoli, you should have robust security arrangements and contingency plans in place."]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/libya/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK who choose to travel against FCDO advice. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Libya’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Libya set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Libyan Embassy in the UK","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/foreign-embassies-in-the-uk"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Libya, your passport must be valid for the duration of your stay."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Previous travel to Israel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If your passport shows previous travel to Israel, it will not be accepted for travel to Libya."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Be aware that a British passport or a certificate of entitlement to the right of abode could attract unwanted attention and pose a risk to your personal safety. Stay alert and exercise vigilance at all times."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel to Libya against FCDO advice, ensure you have the right visa or you may be refused entry. Contact the ",["Libyan Embassy in London","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/foreign-embassies-in-the-uk"]," for further information on the visa you require. You may require a specific type of visa depending on the nature of your trip."]},{"k":"p","s":["A Libyan visa issued overseas may not be recognised in some areas or at certain ports of entry, due to the current political situation."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Libya guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/129/libya#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Registering with the police"]},{"k":"p","s":["All short-stay visitors must register with the police within a week of arrival. The company you’re visiting or the local travel agency you’re using will usually arrange this. If you do not register, you could get a fine when you leave the country."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Libya","https://lawsociety.ly/en/legislation/law-no-10-of-2010-regarding-customs/#Chapter_One_The_Import"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into or out of Libya"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you enter or leave Libya with over 10,000 US dollars-worth of cash, you must declare it to the customs authorities. If you do not, you risk having the money confiscated and even being detained."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a severe shortage of cash in Libya, and the Libyan Central Bank has imposed restrictions on cash withdrawals throughout the country."]},{"k":"p","s":["Libya is a cash-based society. You cannot use bank cards at banks, ATMs or hotels."]},{"k":"p","s":["The exchange of foreign currency into Libyan dinar is tightly controlled by the Libyan Central Bank. Accessing Libyan dinar through formal methods is difficult for travellers. You can legally use exchange offices approved by the Central Bank, but they operate at black market rates. No bank will change money. Informal methods of currency exchange exist though their use carries the risk of arrest."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to take dinar out of Libya."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Libya"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in Libya cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks could be indiscriminate, including in places visited by foreigners. British people and nationals of other western countries are considered high-value hostages. Government buildings and security checkpoints have also been targeted in recent years, leading to deaths."]},{"k":"p","s":["Recent significant attacks include:"]},{"k":"li","s":["In April 2022, 3 people were injured in a car bombing attack by Islamic State near a Tariq Ibn Ziyad Brigade camp in Umm el Araneb, Southern Libya."]},{"k":"li","s":["In 2021 Daesh claimed responsibility for 2 separate bomb attacks on Libyan security forces in the Fezzan province in southern Libya. These attacks killed at least 4 people."]},{"k":"p","s":["Extremist groups including Daesh in Libya (formerly referred to as ISIL) are responsible for most attacks, which have targeted:"]},{"k":"li","s":["foreign and diplomatic personnel and premises"]},{"k":"li","s":["international hotels"]},{"k":"li","s":["oil installations"]},{"k":"li","s":["government and other official security institutions"]},{"k":"p","s":["While there is a greater concentration of terrorist groups in the Fezzan Province, it is highly likely smaller numbers of terrorists can move relatively freely to other towns and regions to facilitate activity outside of the Fezzan Province."]},{"k":"p","s":["In the north of Libya, it is likely security pressure and violence between armed groups have helped to limit the movement of terrorist groups."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is likely both Daesh and Al-Qaida in the Islamic Maghreb (AQIM) are using the Fezzan region in south-west Libya as a base for operations such as people-smuggling and illegally transporting weapons. These resources are essential to support terrorist activity in West Africa."]},{"k":"p","s":["The risk of terrorism may be higher if you travel in the border regions. Extremist groups operating in the region, including AQIM and Daesh, conduct operations in the border areas of northern Mali, Niger and Algeria. They have a proven capability of travelling long distances to carry out attacks, including in neighbouring countries and Libya."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorist kidnap"]},{"k":"p","s":["There has not been a terrorist kidnap in Libya for many years. Terrorist groups within Libya have intent to carry out kidnappings and it’s a realistic possibility that they will target foreign nationals. Criminal gangs also carry out kidnappings, and there is a realistic possibility that they would sell hostages on to terrorist groups. Foreign nationals have been kidnapped in Libya in the past, including 4 foreign nationals in south-east Libya in 2018."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is likely that any kidnap for ransom attempt by a terrorist group in Libya would occur in the south of the country, whereas a kidnap for ransom by militias would occur in a northern coastal city."]},{"k":"p","s":["In recent months, the importance of oil and natural gas production in Libya has increased, and any western nationals working for oil and natural gas companies in Libya are highly likely viewed as high-value targets."]},{"k":"p","s":["British nationals are seen as legitimate targets, including tourists, humanitarian aid workers, journalists and business travellers. If you are kidnapped, the reason for your presence is unlikely to protect you or secure your safe release."]},{"k":"p","s":["The long-standing policy of the British government is to not make substantive concessions to hostage takers. The British government considers that paying ransoms and releasing prisoners builds the capability of terrorist groups and finances their activities. This can, in turn, increase the risk of further hostage-taking. The Terrorism Act (2000) makes payments to terrorists illegal."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["The political situation in Libya is fragile and the security situation remains dangerous and unpredictable."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a UN-facilitated ceasefire in place between eastern and western armed forces. The risk of renewed fighting between them has decreased, but there is still a high risk of localised violence between competing armed groups throughout the country."]},{"k":"p","s":["Violence can break out with little warning."]},{"k":"p","s":["On 12 May, significant armed clashes broke out across Tripoli. A ceasefire was agreed on 14 May and has largely held. The situation remains fragile and a return to violence is possible."]},{"k":"p","s":["In 2022, clashes between armed groups in Tripoli killed 32 people and injured over 150. Clashes again broke out in August 2023 in central Tripoli, resulting in roadblocks and the temporary closure of Mitiga Airport."]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreign forces and mercenaries continue to operate in Libya. The Interim Government of National Unity has limited control of security provision across the country. The lack of political stability has led to security incidents, road and airport closures, and oil blockades."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been protests throughout Libya against the continued political deadlock, lack of basic service provision and corruption."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel to Libya, you should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["monitor the local security situation carefully"]},{"k":"li","s":["remain vigilant"]},{"k":"li","s":["have robust security arrangements and contingency plans in place"]},{"k":"h","s":["Public infrastructure and services"]},{"k":"p","s":["Basic services across Libya vary. Petrol can be difficult to access and there are regularly long queues at fuel stations. There can also be power outages in Tripoli and the south and east, particularly in the summer months."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Crime is widespread across Libya and violent robbery, carjacking and smuggling of fuel, drugs, weapons and people are common across the country and in major cities such as Benghazi and Tripoli. Armed groups and local security services regularly carry out operations against criminal gangs."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Take sensible precautions to protect yourself and your belongings. Foreign nationals may be a target for criminals operating in Libya. Be cautious when travelling throughout the country, particularly at night. You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["consider your route"]},{"k":"li","s":["vary daily routines"]},{"k":"li","s":["hide expensive looking items which could draw unwanted attention"]},{"k":"h","s":["Detention"]},{"k":"p","s":["British people have been detained by Libya’s intelligence services or armed groups, including in Tripoli and Benghazi. You are at higher risk of detention if you work:"]},{"k":"li","s":["in private security"]},{"k":"li","s":["as a journalist"]},{"k":"li","s":["for an NGO"]},{"k":"p","s":["If intelligence services or armed groups detain you in Libya, your health and welfare may be put at serious risk, especially if you have an existing medical condition. The British Embassy will have limited capability to help you or intervene on your behalf. Any help will be even more limited for dual nationals of Libyan origin as you will be in your home country and dual nationality is not recognised by the Libyan authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["By law you must carry your passport. Foreign nationals have been arrested for failing to provide the correct documentation when requested."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Ramadan"]},{"k":"p","s":["Libya is an Islamic country. Respect local traditions, laws and religious customs, especially during the holy month of Ramadan."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ramadan dates vary by year and country. During this time do not:"]},{"k":"li","s":["eat, drink, smoke or chew gum in public in the daytime, including in your car"]},{"k":"li","s":["play loud music or dance"]},{"k":"li","s":["swear in public"]},{"k":"p","s":["Get more advice when you arrive from your hotel or business contacts."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check opening hours of shops and restaurants"]},{"k":"li","s":["be aware that if hotels and restaurants are providing food or drink in fasting hours, they may separate you from Islamic guests, for example with screens"]},{"k":"li","s":["follow local dress codes – clothing that does not meet local dress codes may cause more offence at this time"]},{"k":"li","s":["be aware that fasting can cause tiredness, particularly during the later afternoon and early evening"]},{"k":"li","s":["be patient and show tolerance"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Religion"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal in Libya to make any attempt to convert Muslims to another religion."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Alcohol and drugs laws"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal in Libya to drink alcohol or use drugs. Libyan authorities treat drug and alcohol offences seriously. You can be punished for use, possession and trafficking of drugs or alcohol."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Methanol poisoning"]},{"k":"p","s":["There has been an increase in cases of serious illness caused by alcoholic drinks containing methanol around the world."]},{"k":"p","s":["Even small amounts of methanol can kill. It is not possible to identify methanol in alcoholic drinks by taste or smell. See ",["Travel Aware Drink Spiking and Methanol Poisoning","https://travelaware.campaign.gov.uk/spiking-and-methanol-poisoning/"]," for information about how to reduce the risks."]},{"k":"p","s":["Seek urgent medical attention if you or someone you are travelling with show the ",["signs of methanol poisoning","https://travelaware.campaign.gov.uk/spiking-and-methanol-poisoning/"]," after drinking."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Business travel in Libya"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are often complex bureaucratic procedures needed to travel for work in Libya. Follow all relevant protocols and procedures if you intend to travel around Libya for business. You are likely to be at risk of intimidation, harassment and detention if you fail to comply with local customs."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Journalism in Libya"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must get press accreditation from the relevant government authorities. This may reduce the risk of harassment, intimidation and detention by armed groups and Libya’s intelligence services. There is a real risk of hostility from those who object to media reporting. International and local journalists have previously been detained in Libya."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Using cameras near military facilities"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must get permission before taking any photographs or interviewing at or near military facilities."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Consensual same-sex sexual activity is illegal for men and women in Libya, with penalties of up to five years imprisonment."]},{"k":"p","s":["Libyan society is intolerant of people of the same sex showing affection in public. Individuals perceived to be part of the LGBT+ community have experienced physical and psychological violence and harassment. There have also been arrests for people perceived as ‘promoting homosexuality’."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Road travel within Libya remains highly dangerous. There is a risk of being caught up in outbreaks of localised violence between armed groups. There is a high risk of:"]},{"k":"li","s":["carjacking"]},{"k":"li","s":["robbery"]},{"k":"li","s":["striking unexploded bombs or mines off-road"]},{"k":"p","s":["The risk is very high on the coastal road between Zawiyah and Tripoli."]},{"k":"p","s":["All land borders into Libya are subject to closure at short notice."]},{"k":"p","s":["If the security situation worsens, routes in and out of major cities and towns may be blocked and airports closed or made inaccessible at little or no notice."]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not share your travel or other plans through social media. Vary your travel routes and always keep a low profile."]},{"k":"p","s":["Road traffic accidents are common and often result in deaths. The emergency response to a road traffic accident may be limited."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Air travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Flights to and from all airports in Libya are at risk of cancellation at short notice."]},{"k":"p","s":["Tripoli International Airport has been closed since 2014, following clashes between armed groups in the area surrounding the airport. Other airports may change their flight schedule without notice. Mitiga Airport, which operates international flights, is Tripoli’s only currently functioning civilian airport. Fighting between armed groups in the area caused Mitiga Airport to temporarily close in 2023."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["UK Air Safety List (ASL)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/uk-air-safety-list"]," lists all known airlines in Libya that do not meet international safety standards and are banned from operating commercial air services to, from, and within the UK. Check the UK Air Safety List when considering which airlines to fly with. The list is maintained by the Department for Transport, based on advice from the ",["UK Civil Aviation Authority","https://www.caa.co.uk/Commercial-Industry/Airlines/Licensing/Requirements-and-guidance/Third-Country-Operator-Certificates"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Storm damage in eastern Libya"]},{"k":"p","s":["In September 2023 Storm Daniel struck eastern Libya, causing severe flooding and significantly damaging critical infrastructure, including hospitals and telecommunications networks. Libya remains vulnerable to severe weather incidents, with flooding impacting cities, including Tripoli and Misrata in December 2024."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Libya. It only covers regions where the Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office (FCDO) has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/libya"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/libya/safety-and-security"]," for Libya."]},{"k":"h","s":["Tripoli"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to all areas within the city limits of Tripoli."]},{"k":"p","s":["The general security situation in Tripoli has been relatively stable since the UN-brokered ceasefire was signed in October 2020. Areas of the city are controlled by different militia, and the potential for sporadic inter-militia fighting remains, as witnessed in August 2022, during the large-scale mobilisation in May 2023 and clashes in August 2023 and May 2025. Such clashes can break out without warning and may involve exchanges of high-calibre gunfire and rocket propelled grenades."]},{"k":"h","s":["Benghazi"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to all areas within the city limits of Benghazi. Al-Bayda and Derna, and the region of Jebel Akhdar, either side of the coastal and desert roads between (and including) the towns of Al-Maqrun and Al-Tamimi."]},{"k":"p","s":["The general security situation in Benghazi has been calm throughout 2024 and 2025. Security is controlled by a single military group, however there remains a risk of in-fighting, but there have not been any incidents in the last 12 months."]},{"k":"h","s":["Misrata"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to all areas of within the city limits of Misrata."]},{"k":"p","s":["The security situation in Misrata is stable despite the presence in the city of several armed groups. Regular policing is the responsibility of the Misrata Security Directorate, the security forces are widely assessed to be the most professional in the western region."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 1415 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Medical help in remote areas may not be available. Even if your travel or insurance company has arrangements with an international air ambulance provider, they may not be allowed to carry out a rescue operation within Libya. The current status of Libyan rescue services is uncertain."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Libya","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/129/libya#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Libya","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/129/libya"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["leishmaniasis"]},{"k":"li","s":["West Nile virus"]},{"k":"li","s":["schistosomiasis"]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Libya"]},{"k":"p","s":["Healthcare in Libya is generally below the standard available in the UK. There are acute shortages of skilled personnel and medical supplies, particularly for chronic conditions such as diabetes. There are private clinics in Tripoli."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you need treatment you may be evacuated to Malta or mainland Europe. Make sure you have adequate travel health insurance and accessible funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad and repatriation."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Libya"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 1415 (ambulance, fire, police)"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/libya/british-embassy-tunis"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/libya/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]}],"chars":22415,"change":"FCDO now advises against all but essential travel to the cities of Tripoli, Benghazi, Al-Bayda and Derna, and the surrounding area knows as Jebel Akhdar, either side of the coastal and desert roads between and including the towns of Al-Maqrun and Al-Tamimi, Misrata ('Warnings and insurance' and 'Reg"},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/seychelles":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:18Z","sha":"1a2481f24a6dffb1007b04de66a6b9dacec9e3497739f291225466e86fcd3418","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/seychelles","bytes":52950,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Seychelles travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-01-21T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-10-23T16:17:13+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/seychelles/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Seychelles’ current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Seychelles set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Seychelles High Commission in the UK","https://mfa.gov.sc/seychelles-representation-abroad-diplomatic-missions-united-kingdom-northern-ireland/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re visiting Seychelles, your passport must be valid for the duration of your stay."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You do not need a visa to visit Seychelles, but you must get a travel authorisation before you travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["When you arrive, you’ll get a visitor’s permit for 3 months. You can apply for 3-month extensions, up to a maximum period of 12 months in total, if you meet ",["visitor permit extension requirements","https://www.ics.gov.sc/permits/visitors-permit"],". You can ",["apply online to extend before your current visitor’s permit expires","https://www.permits.ics.gov.sc/en"]," or in-person at the Immigration and Civil Status Office in Victoria."]},{"k":"p","s":["The visitor’s permit is free for the first 3 months, but there is a fee for each 3-month extension."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Applying for a travel authorisation"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must ",["apply and pay for a travel authorisation online","https://seychelles.govtas.com/"]," to enter Seychelles. You can apply up to 30 days before you arrive. In an emergency, you can pay an extra fee for an express application."]},{"k":"p","s":["When you apply, you will need to show you meet Seychelles entry requirements. These include having:"]},{"k":"li","s":["a valid return or onward ticket"]},{"k":"li","s":["proof of confirmed accommodation"]},{"k":"li","s":["sufficient funds for the duration of your stay"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Checks at border control"]},{"k":"p","s":["When you arrive, you may need to show proof you have travel insurance and are covered for any potential health costs."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination if you’re coming from a ",["country listed as a transmission risk","https://nathnacyfzone.org.uk/factsheet/65/countries-with-risk-of-yellow-fever-transmission"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For full details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Seychelles guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/194/seychelles#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health screening"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Ebola"]},{"k":"p","s":["As a precaution, Seychelles has introduced additional health measures in response to the Ebola outbreak."]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreign nationals must spend at least 21 days outside the Democratic Republic of Congo and meet monitoring requirements before they can enter Seychelles."]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreign nationals who have been in, or transited through, the Democratic Republic of Congo in the last 21 days, may be screened on arrival in Seychelles and not allowed to enter."]},{"k":"p","s":["Seychellois citizens and residents may enter within the 21-day limit, but must comply with mandatory health measures, including possible quarantine."]},{"k":"p","s":["For more information, contact the Seychelles ",["Ministry of Health","https://www.health.gov.sc/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Seychelles","https://src.gov.sc/customs-and-excises/#Passenger_Guide"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into Seychelles"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are taking cash with a value of 50,000 Seychellois rupees or more into Seychelles, declare this when you apply for your travel authorisation."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Seychelles"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there is no recent history of terrorism in Seychelles, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting yourself and your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a risk of break-ins, robberies, burglaries and opportunistic thefts. Crime is generally non-violent, but people can have their bags snatched, cars and boats broken into or be robbed while walking at night."]},{"k":"p","s":["Criminals may target parked cars, residential accommodation including guest houses, hotels, beaches, and marked and unmarked walking trails."]},{"k":"p","s":["To reduce the risk to yourself and your belongings:"]},{"k":"li","s":["make sure your living accommodation has external security lighting, grilles and overnight security guards"]},{"k":"li","s":["use a hotel safe or safety deposit box to store valuables, money and passports"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not leave valuables in cars or anywhere on display"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid carrying large amounts of cash or wearing expensive-looking jewellery or watches"]},{"k":"li","s":["carry a mobile phone with roaming capability for use in an emergency"]},{"k":"p","s":["Be particularly alert in:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Beau Vallon"]},{"k":"li","s":["the back streets of Victoria"]},{"k":"li","s":["any isolated area, especially at night"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call the Seychelles police on +248 428 8000 to report any incident."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Public nudity"]},{"k":"p","s":["Topless sunbathing is uncommon. Nudism is not accepted and is likely to cause offence."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["Seychelles has strict laws on drug use and smuggling. Penalties are severe and may include fines and prison sentences, up to life imprisonment. This can be applied both for supplying and simple possession of any drugs, including when transiting through the airport. Bail is highly unlikely and prosecutions can take a year or more to come to court."]},{"k":"p","s":["Seychelles International Airport has excellent technology and security for detecting illegal items, including cannabis. This can be used to scan the baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Pack all luggage yourself and never carry anything through customs for anyone else."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual activity is legal in Seychelles, but attitudes vary. Same-sex marriage is not recognised."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Swimming"]},{"k":"p","s":["Beaches can be safe for swimming at some times of the year and dangerous at others. In general, the west coast is unsafe during the north-west monsoon (around December to March), and the east coast is unsafe during the south-east winds season (around May to September). Beaches at the southern tip are not suitable for swimming at any time."]},{"k":"p","s":["Beaches do not always have safety information. You should not assume they’re safe. The popular Beau Vallon beach, and some other beaches, may have dangerous rip currents when the sea is rough."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are not many lifeguards, though some are stationed on popular beaches. Ask for local advice about the conditions before you visit the beaches and follow any warning signs."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["water safety on holiday","https://www.rlss.org.uk/safety-on-holiday"]," from the Royal Life Saving Society."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Hiking"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is generally safe to hike in Seychelles. Visitors have sometimes got lost on nature walks or hiking on trails. You can plan a route and access online ",["guides and safety tips from the Seychelles Tourism Department","https://tourism.gov.sc/?cat_doc=safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Seychelles, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],". You can see the ",["legal requirements you need to be aware of","https://seylii.org/akn/sc/act/1935/24/eng@2016-08-01#sec_24"]," in the Road Transport Act."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Seychelles for 3 months. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"]," or get a ",["1968 international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," as well."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience, a higher minimum age and holding an IDP."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving and road standards"]},{"k":"p","s":["Mahé is mountainous, and roads are narrow and winding, often with sheer drops and hairpin bends. Not all such roads are equipped with safety barriers. Deep, uncovered storm drains flank many roads."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drink-driving is a problem, so other road users may behave erratically."]},{"k":"p","s":["Sudden heavy downpours can reduce visibility and road surface conditions quickly. Potholes can appear suddenly after heavy rains. Drivers sometimes veer sharply across lanes to avoid them."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Public transport"]},{"k":"p","s":["Buses are cheap but infrequent on some routes. A timetable is available from the bus station in Victoria or from the ",["Seychelles Public Transport Corporation","https://sptc.sc/timetable/"],". Most public buses do not operate after 8pm."]},{"k":"p","s":["Taxis are generally of a good standard. Taxi meters are increasingly common but if a taxi is not equipped with one, agree a fare before starting your journey."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sea travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Piracy in the region has decreased but there is still some risk, especially off the coast of Somalia. You can check the latest situation with United Kingdom Maritime Trade Operations and Maritime Security Centre (Horn of Africa)."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling by sea, ",["read about the regional risks at sea","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/sea-river-and-piracy-safety"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Most of the inner island resorts are accessible by ferry. Pay attention to safety briefings when taking any boat trips. Make sure life jackets are provided, especially on smaller excursion boats."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to prepare for and respond to ",["extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Heavy rain and severe weather"]},{"k":"p","s":["Heavy or prolonged rainfall can cause flooding, landslides, falling trees, and poor visibility, particularly during the months from November to March. Severe weather can cause damage to property and infrastructure. Roads may become damaged or blocked, leading to closures and travel disruption. Domestic flights and ferries may also be disrupted."]},{"k":"p","s":["Monitor weather updates from the ",["Seychelles Meteorological Authority","https://www.meteo.sc/"]," and local media, and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["Call the ",["Department of Risk and Disaster Management","https://www.drdm.sc/"]," on 160 to report flooding, landslides, infrastructure disruptions or other related incidents. For life-threatening emergencies, call 999, 151 or 111."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drought"]},{"k":"p","s":["Seychelles experiences periods of severe drought. The authorities may impose water restrictions during these periods. Follow local guidance and use water sensibly."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 999 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Seychelles","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/194/seychelles#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Seychelles","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/194/seychelles"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Seychelles"]},{"k":"p","s":["As a tourist, you must pay for medical treatment."]},{"k":"p","s":["Residents in Seychelles from overseas need to pay for some treatment, including medication and prescriptions."]},{"k":"p","s":["The main hospital is at Mont Fleuri in Victoria on Mahé, and there are local health centres in most residential areas. Victoria Hospital has an accident and emergency department, and facilities such as scans. There are many private clinics in Seychelles, mostly offering GP services."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Seychelles","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/seychelles-list-of-medical-facilitiespractitioners"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Seychelles"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 999 (ambulance, fire, police)"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=seychelles"]," and ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=seychelles"]," in Seychelles"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Seychelles","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/seychelles-bereavement-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Seychelles","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/seychelles-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/seychelles/british-high-commission-victoria"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/seychelles/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Seychelles","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-seychelles"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":14245,"change":"New information about Ebola health measures for entry into Seychelles (‘Entry requirements’ page)."},"https://theconversation.com/when-government-fails-who-helps-citizens-get-answers-how-south-africas-community-developers-bridge-the-gap-293556":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T17:32Z","sha":"68daaaea8ed08af2cd437b90ca5a969010d981185daf3b472065c983905646a8","read":"https://theconversation.com/when-government-fails-who-helps-citizens-get-answers-how-south-africas-community-developers-bridge-the-gap-293556","bytes":178077,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"When government fails, who helps citizens get answers? How South Africa’s community developers bridge the gap","by":[{"n":"Lesedi Senamele Matlala","r":"Monitoring & Evaluation Scholar-Practitioner, University of Johannesburg"}],"pub":"2026-11-03T14:08:51Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["South Africans – like many citizens across the world – don’t experience their government as a single entity."]},{"k":"p","s":["Responsibility for water, housing, social grants, clinics and waste collection is distributed across different spheres, departments, municipalities and public offices. A resident can know exactly what has gone wrong without knowing who has the authority to fix it, where to report it or what to do when nobody responds."]},{"k":"p","s":["In these situations, the role of community developers can be crucial. They help residents organise, understand government procedures and raise concerns with the relevant authorities. Their work is recognised internationally through ",["community development practice standards","https://www.iacdglobal.org/international_standards_for_community_development"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["In South Africa, community development practitioners form part of a wider social service workforce. Their professional registration framework is ",["still being developed","https://www.sacssp.co.za/sites/default/files/content-files/Policy%20%26%20Publications/Notices%20%26%20Statements/Board%20Notices/2026/SACSSP%20NationalGov%20Pages.pdf#page=2"],", but in 2024, the ",["government reported employing 23,561 social service professionals","https://www.gov.za/news/media-advisories/conferences-summits-seminars-and-workshops/minister-nokuzola-tolashe"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["I am a ",["public policy expert","https://www.uj.ac.za/members/dr-lesedi-matlala/"]," focusing on monitoring, evaluation and citizen participation. In a ",["recent study","https://doi.org/10.1080/21665095.2026.2706526"],", I set out to gain insights into what community developers actually do to help communities navigate government bureaucracy. And how they raise concerns about public services."]},{"k":"p","s":["I interviewed 12 community developers working in government, non-governmental organisations and civil society in six of South Africa’s nine provinces. They included:"]},{"k":"li","s":["community development practitioners who support local development projects and participation"]},{"k":"li","s":["community mobilisers who bring residents together around shared concerns"]},{"k":"li","s":["programme coordinators and monitoring facilitators who organise community activities and follow up on their progress."]},{"k":"p","s":["Each had between five and 20 years of experience. Rather than focusing only on their formal responsibilities, I wanted to understand how their role worked in practice."]},{"k":"p","s":["I ",["identified","https://doi.org/10.1080/21665095.2026.2706526"]," several ways they bridged the gap between citizens and government. They identified a local problem, helped people organise around it and then connected community action with government institutions and resources."]},{"k":"p","s":["They also explained government systems and communicated community concerns to state officials."]},{"k":"p","s":["The community developers weren’t able to fix every failed public service, or force government departments to respond. But they did make it easier for citizens to navigate government – and harder for government to overlook community experiences."]},{"k":"p","s":["This connecting role deserves more attention from municipalities and government departments."]},{"k":"p","s":["Community developers convene residents, help document complaints and approach officials or service providers who can respond. Their work often involves meetings and follow-up visits."]},{"k":"h","s":["Navigating a complicated state"]},{"k":"p","s":["My ",["research","https://doi.org/10.1080/21665095.2026.2706526"]," identified five roles that community developers play."]},{"k":"p","s":["First, they mobilise residents. For example, in the ",["Sondela informal settlement in Rustenburg","https://www.gov.za/news/media-statements/north-west-cooperative-governance-and-traditional-affairs-role-community"],", a city in the North West province, one community development worker helped support the ⁠ ",["Phepafatso Project","https://phepafatso.org.za/"],". This is an initiative to address waste and sanitation problems."]},{"k":"p","s":["The initiative got residents together around clean-ups and waste separation through community and school-based awareness activities. They converted illegal dumping areas into vegetable gardens and developed sanitation solutions to improve basic hygiene. Supported at first by Doctors Without Borders, the project later received support through the provincial government’s community development programme."]},{"k":"p","s":["Second, community developers explain government systems to residents. Community developers were able to “translate” between these two worlds. As one respondent ",["explained","https://doi.org/10.1080/21665095.2026.2706526"],":"]},{"k":"p","s":["Many people know that services are not working properly, but they do not know where to report or how the system works. Our job is to explain how the process works and who is responsible."]},{"k":"p","s":["Third, they communicate community concerns to officials. They help residents understand which institution is responsible and how government processes work. They also help officials understand what service failure looks like from the community’s perspective."]},{"k":"p","s":["Fourth, they help manage interactions between citizens and public institutions. Once participant ",["described","https://doi.org/10.1080/21665095.2026.2706526"]," the difficulty of making official information usable:"]},{"k":"p","s":["The documents from the government are often complicated. We have to break them down and explain what they mean so that people can understand what services they are supposed to receive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Another described helping communities document issues so they could be followed up with the responsible department. Without this process, they explained, complaints could easily be forgotten."]},{"k":"p","s":["This translation also works in the opposite direction. Communities don’t always use the administrative language of government. They describe what they experience, often after living with a problem for some time."]},{"k":"p","s":["One practitioner ",["explained","https://doi.org/10.1080/21665095.2026.2706526"],":"]},{"k":"p","s":["People sometimes express their frustrations emotionally because they are affected by service delivery problems. Part of our work is to organise those concerns in a way that government officials can understand and respond to."]},{"k":"p","s":["Fifth, they follow up on issues that residents have raised and referred to officials."]},{"k":"h","s":["Keeping the state engaged"]},{"k":"p","s":["Community developers cannot make up for public institutions that don’t respond."]},{"k":"p","s":["Study participants ",["reported","https://doi.org/10.1080/21665095.2026.2706526"]," inadequate resources for transport, venues and follow-up activities from their employing organisations and programmes. They described the difficulty of maintaining community confidence when government departments failed to respond to concerns."]},{"k":"p","s":["This distinction is important. A community developer can help residents identify the correct department, document a problem and communicate it. The job of repairing a road, resolving a water interruption or delivering a public service still belongs to the institution."]},{"k":"p","s":["South Africa – particularly national and provincial departments and municipalities – should treat community developers as part of the connective infrastructure of government. They should not be seen just as people who organise meetings or mobilise communities."]},{"k":"p","s":["This means providing training and resources to do the work. It also requires clear systems for referring community concerns to responsible officials, tracking them and answering them."]}],"chars":6230}};
